Let $\mathbf{A}$ be a noetherian ring, and $\mathbf{I}$ an ideal in $\mathbf{A}$, say $\mathbf{I} 
eq \mathbf{A}$. For each element $f \in \mathbf{A}$, we define the symbol $
u_{\mathrm{I}}(f)$ as follows: $
u_{\mathrm{I}}(f)$ is the largest integer $
u \geq 0$ such that $f \in \mathbf{I}^{
u}$, if it exists, and $
u_{\mathrm{I}}(f) = \infty$ if $f \in \mathbf{I}^{
u}$ for all positive integers $
u$. If $\mathbf{A}$ is a local ring, then $
u_{\mathrm{I}}(f) = \infty$ implies that $f = 0$. If $
u = 
u_{\mathrm{I}}(f)$ is a non-negative integer, then the class of $f$ in $\mathbf{I}^{
u}/\mathbf{I}^{
u+1}$ will be called the initial form of $f$ in $\mathrm{gr}_{\mathrm{I}}(\mathbf{A})$. For the sake of convenience, we define the initial form of $f$ with $
u_{\mathrm{I}}(f) = \infty$ to be the zero element of $\mathrm{gr}_{\mathrm{I}}(\mathbf{A})$.
